# A parenthesized sub-expression dissolves into its tree; each renderer writes the minimal pair —
# Dev, 2026-09-21 ("Next parser steps as you suggest"), the step named at the close of 665926e
# (625 / 44 green). GitHub #61.
#
# ONE CONCEPT: parentheses are a SURFACE, not a node. A `( )` group with nothing before it and no
# depth-0 comma used to be an opaque leaf; now `treed` reads its inside as a value, and when that
# is a tree the group DISSOLVES into it — a tree is already associated, so the pair carries nothing.
# A group the parser reads as a leaf keeps its parentheses (`(x: T)`, a host `if`), because there
# the pair may be the host's. The renderers then write the MINIMAL pair, each by its OWN target's
# fixity: Drake.render by drake's table (a looser child; an equally tight child on the side its
# associativity does not cover; a lambda or conditional only when something follows it, since the
# arrow's fixity lets it run to the end; the receiver of a path or the head of an application when
# it is any of those). DracoGenerator.render by Scala's precedence — first character, all grouping
# left, `++` ranked WITH `+` where drake ranks it below — and a lambda, arrow or conditional always,
# since none of them is an infix operand in Scala. So `xs ++ (n + 1)` keeps its pair in Scala and
# loses it in drake, `(a || b) && c` keeps it on both, `a || (b && c)` loses it on both. The same
# tree, parenthesized per target: the fixity that was "a surface concern only" (dlt) now has both
# surfaces to prove it.
#
